\title{Custom Preprocessing}
\description{
Applies a user-supplied function \code{fn} to (one or more) \code{\link{lazy_tensor}} columns of a task.
It is the preprocessing analogue of \code{\link[=mlr_pipeops_nn_fn]{nn("fn")}} and an alternative to creating a new class via
\code{\link[=pipeop_preproc_torch]{pipeop_preproc_torch()}}.

The function receives the tensor as its first argument and must return a tensor.
Its other arguments are the parameters of the \code{PipeOp}, see section Parameters.

Being a \code{trafo_} operator, its \code{stages} parameter starts out as \code{"both"}, so the transformation is applied both
while training and when predicting.
For an augmentation that is only applied while training, use \code{\link[=mlr_pipeops_augment_fn]{po("augment_fn")}}.
See \code{\link{PipeOpTaskPreprocTorch}} for the \code{stages} parameter and this naming convention.
}
\section{Parameters}{

By default, the parameters are inferred as all arguments of \code{fn} except for the first one (and \code{...}).
They are untyped and tagged \code{"train"}, and those without a default value are tagged \code{"required"} as well.
To specify them more explicitly, e.g. to make them tunable, use the construction argument \code{param_set}.
In addition, there are the parameters \code{stages} and \code{affect_columns}, see \code{\link{PipeOpTaskPreprocTorch}}.
}

\section{Shapes}{

By default (\code{shapes_out = "infer"}), the output shape is inferred by applying \code{fn} to an empty tensor of the input
shape, see \code{\link[=pipeop_preproc_torch]{pipeop_preproc_torch()}}.
This assumes that the output shape only depends on the input shape and the parameter values.
When this is not the case, e.g. because the output shape is random, set \code{shapes_out} to \code{NULL}, which declares
the output shape as unknown.
\code{\link[=mlr_pipeops_torch_ingress_ltnsr]{po("torch_ingress_ltnsr")}} then needs its \code{shape} parameter to be set.
}

